        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "While assuming the constraint that a reasonable solution must _not_ force us to keep track of duplicate line state as rendered in the directory buffer (see [here](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/39723#discussion_r3410299742)), I propose leveraging an autocmd that allows the user to tap into the state of dir.lua _generally_, not just for sorting entries:\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('User', {\n  pattern = 'NvimDirPreRender',\n  callback = function(ev)\n    local mtimes = {}\n\n    for _, entry in ipairs(ev.data.entries) do\n      local stat = entry.path and vim.uv.fs_stat(entry.path)\n      mtimes[entry] = stat and stat.mtime.sec or 0\n    end\n\n    table.sort(ev.data.entries, function(a, b)\n      return mtimes[a] > mtimes[b]\n    end)\n  end,\n})\n```\n\nAlternatives include exposing a `list_entries` function to the [prospective driver api](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40482):\n\n```lua\nprovider.list_entries(ctx, function(err, entries)\n  if provider.transform_entries then\n    entries = provider.transform_entries(ctx, entries) or entries\n  end\n\n  vim.api.nvim_exec_autocmds('User', {\n    pattern = 'NvimDirPreRender',\n    data = { buf = ctx.buf, name = ctx.name, entries = entries },\n  })\n\n  render_entries(ctx.buf, ctx.name, entries)\n  ctx.entries = entries\nend)\n```\n\nI think the latter is at the wrong abstraction level; a provider method helps provider authors; a `User` autocmd helps users configuring the built-in provider.\n\nLastly, I think hard-coding config for this like `vim.g.nvim_dir_sort` is useless for core code to shoulder all permutations of popular sorting methodologies on (which have been proven, in my configging over the years, to be multitudinous).\n\nOpen to other ideas.\n",
